From 62210b34a8e8e86a3580f5c261159328317fe671 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 05:29:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(shell): wire the three storage operations nothing called

NR-STO-07, NR-STO-15 and NR-STO-16 were specified, agreed at review, and
implemented in storage/ - where test_storage_py.py covers them. Nothing on the
page ever called them. The functions passed their tests, the figures were right,
so nothing failed; what was missing was a caller, and an operation nobody calls
is a requirement nobody met.

NR-STO-16 - a plan that changed on disk beneath a reader
  A session that has only READ a plan never takes a claim, so nothing stopped it
  saving over a colleague's newer save - and the save reported success. Two
  guards now, because a guard only in the window is one anything else walks past:
  - bridge.js polls the plan every 10s (S-N01, AGREED), enters STALE, offers
    File -> Reload plan, and refuses an edit or a save from a superseded view.
  - storage/workspace.py refuses the write itself when the caller states which
    issue of the file its figures came from and the file is a different issue.
  What is compared is the plan's own last_saved, not its modification time: a
  share may round a modification time to two seconds, and a check that cannot
  tell two saves apart is no check. ws/stat now reports lastSaved for this.

NR-STO-07 - pending edits survive a crash
  journal/write was never called, so journal/read had nothing to find and the
  recovery offer could never appear. The pending count is now WATCHED rather
  than hooked: S.pending is changed in a dozen places in ui/, and a journal that
  depends on having wrapped each of them is a journal with holes in it.

NR-STO-15 - a claim ends when the holder is finished
  Only the application-close path released. Now also: 'Leave without change',
  opening another plan, and New plan. stop_heartbeat() actually stops the beat -
  it set an attribute to None while the thread went on refreshing the old plan's
  claim, so a plan the session had left stayed locked until its 30-minute expiry.
  The discard hook is a click LISTENER, not a wrapper: shell/web binds the button
  to the function itself, so replacing the global would have left the button
  calling the original.

Also in this change
  - workspace.py SCHEMA_EXPECTED 5 -> 12. core/00_meta.js reads 12, so the
    storage layer was stamping a version the engine had never used. The test
    that "checked" this compared the mistake with itself; it now reads the
    number out of core/00_meta.js so the two cannot drift again.
  - The four operations that open a file dialog no longer run holding the single
    application lock. A dialog waits up to ten minutes for a person; every other
    request queued behind it, the page's own claim check included, and the window
    looked frozen because it was. ws/saveAs takes the lock around the part that
    touches shared state.
  - The change log is one file per person per month in the installation's one
    audit folder. Two sessions appending to one file across an SMB share can
    interleave: Python buffers text and a share does not promise an atomic
    append. Separate files cannot interleave, and every row already names who
    made the change, so a month still reads as one record.
  - paths.py gains shared_dir(): data/shared/workspaces/, created at launch with
    a note in it. 07_Sharing is written for several people opening one plan, and
    rule 3 puts every plan in one person's private folder - where a claim, a
    heartbeat and a who-is-editing message protect nothing, because nobody else
    can open the file.

Verification
  test_python_app.py gains 14 checks for the wiring itself - that the page calls
  each operation, and that each one does what its requirement says: the claim
  comes back on release, on discard and on opening another plan; an edit is
  journalled and the journal is cleared on commit; a colleague's save is noticed
  AND THE SAVE IS REFUSED with their file intact. These are the checks whose
  absence let the gap persist.

  build_python_app.py, test_layers.py, test_storage_py.py (80 passed) and
  test_python_app.py (0 failed) all pass. The two-instance reproduction that
  demonstrated the loss - two shells on one shared folder, A saves twelve
  projects, B saves a three-project view over the top - now ends in a refusal
  with all twelve intact.

